        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Treadmill Repair Near Me: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If your treadmill is still under any part of its warranty, the right treadmill
            repair service is the manufacturer's: contact the brand's support team first, with the
            serial number and proof of purchase, and let them send an authorised technician. If it
            is out of warranty, an independent fitness-equipment technician is usually the best
            value, provided they work on treadmills regularly and diagnose before ordering
            parts.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            A technician visit in the US typically has a call-out or diagnostic fee somewhere in
            the region of $75 to $150, with labour and the part on top. Common technician repairs,
            such as a drive belt, a speed sensor or motor brushes, often come to roughly $120 to
            $300 all in; a motor control board or drive motor with a visit is commonly in the region
            of $300 to $900. These are the same estimated ranges used in our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>,
            and they are typical bands, not quotes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before you search at all, do the free checks: the safety key, the outlet, the breaker,
            deck lubrication and belt tension. A large share of treadmill problems turn out to be
            one of those, and a repairman who drives out to find a dry deck still charges the
            call-out fee. The rest of this page explains how to choose between repairers, what to
            have ready, what to ask, and what should make you look elsewhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-you-search" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Do You Need a Repairman at All?</h2>
<p>
            Many treadmill faults that feel like a repair job are owner fixes, so rule those out
            first. It costs nothing and it changes what you tell a technician if you do book one.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Will not power on.</strong> Check the safety key is fully seated, the
            outlet works with another appliance, the treadmill's own reset button or circuit
            breaker (often near the power switch) has not tripped, and the household breaker is on.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill troubleshooting guide</a>
            works through this in order.</li>
            <li><strong>Belt slips or hesitates.</strong> Usually a dry deck or a loose belt, both
            fixed at home in under an hour.</li>
            <li><strong>Belt drifts to one side.</strong> A tracking adjustment at the rear roller,
            also an owner job.</li>
            <li><strong>Squeaks and rubbing.</strong> Often a belt touching a rail, a loose bolt or
            a dry deck.</li>
            <li><strong>Console glitches.</strong> A full power cycle with the key out, and
            reseating the console cable connector at the base of the upright, fixes more than it
            should.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Book a technician when the fault is electrical or mechanical beyond adjustment: a
            breaker that trips repeatedly, a burning smell, a motor that will not turn, a belt that
            surges or stops at random, an incline that will not move, an error code that returns
            after a reset, or grinding from the motor or rollers. Those point to a motor, a control
            board, a sensor, an incline motor or bearings, and they are worth a professional
            diagnosis.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Is It Still Under Warranty? Check Before You Call Anyone</h2>
<p>
            Check the warranty before booking any repair, because a part or labour that is still
            covered becomes a support call rather than a bill, and an unauthorised repair can affect
            that cover.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treadmill warranties are usually split into separate terms for the frame, the motor,
            parts and labour, and the terms differ by brand and model. Labour is commonly the
            shortest. A machine three years old may be out of labour cover but still in motor or
            parts cover, in which case the manufacturer may ship the part free and you pay only for
            fitting. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how those tiers usually work.
          </p>
          <p>
            Things that commonly decide whether the warranty helps:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Original purchaser only.</strong> Many treadmill warranties do not transfer
            to a second owner. Sole's support pages, for example, state that its warranties apply
            to the original purchaser only. A used machine may have no cover regardless of its
            age.</li>
            <li><strong>Proof of purchase and registration.</strong> Keep the receipt. Some brands
            ask for registration within a set period after purchase.</li>
            <li><strong>Authorised service.</strong> Manufacturers generally expect warranty repairs
            to be done by their own or authorised technicians. Having an independent repairer
            replace a board on an in-warranty machine can give the manufacturer grounds to decline
            later claims on that part.</li>
            <li><strong>Maintenance.</strong> A worn deck or failed controller on a machine that was
            never lubricated may be treated as neglect. A note of lubrication dates helps.</li>
            <li><strong>Extended plans.</strong> If you bought a protection plan from the brand or
            the retailer, it usually has its own claims route and its own in-home service network.
            Use it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The practical rule: if any part of any warranty or plan might apply, make the brand or
            plan support call before the repair call.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="manufacturer-service" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How Do Manufacturer Service Networks Work?</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmill brands do not employ a technician in every town. They run a support team
            that troubleshoots by phone or online, ships parts, and dispatches either their own
            technicians or contracted independent service companies for in-home work. The process
            usually starts with you, not the technician.
          </p>
          <p>
            Below is how three of the larger brands describe their routes on their own sites,
            checked on 14 October 2026. Contact details and processes change, so use the brand's
            current support page rather than a number from an old manual.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">NordicTrack and ProForm (iFIT)</h3>
          <p>
            NordicTrack's published service-plan terms describe a claim that starts by contacting
            NordicTrack by phone or online, followed by troubleshooting with one of its repair
            specialists. If that does not resolve it, replacement parts are shipped from
            NordicTrack and a technician is then dispatched to install them. Owner reviews on
            independent sites describe the same pattern and note that in-home work is often carried
            out by contracted repair companies, which is where most complaints about waiting times
            arise. Expect a remote diagnosis first and a visit second.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sole</h3>
          <p>
            Sole's support pages direct owners to submit a service and warranty request with the
            serial number and purchase information to hand. The serial number on Sole treadmills is
            on a sticker on the front of the lower base, and Sole asks for every number and letter
            on it. Sole's own FAQ also asks buyers in rural or hard-to-service areas to contact it
            before purchasing so it can confirm that a service provider covers their area, which is
            a useful reminder that in-home service depends on local coverage for every brand.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Horizon (Johnson Health Tech)</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon's support FAQ directs existing owners to its in-warranty customer tech team,
            and asks for the model number, serial number, a description of the problem including
            any error message, and contact details. It states that proof of purchase is required
            for warranty claims and that Horizon has partnered with independent service providers
            across the United States for in-home service where needed. Horizon's owner's manuals
            say service should be performed only by an authorised Horizon service provider.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Out of warranty with the brand.</strong> Many brands will still sell you parts
            and, in some areas, refer you to their authorised service companies for paid repairs.
            Asking the brand who services their machines locally is one of the most reliable ways
            to find a technician who knows your model.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recalls.</strong> Before paying for any repair, search the recalls list on
            cpsc.gov, the Consumer Product Safety Commission's site, for your brand and model. If the fault matches a recall, the remedy is free from the
            manufacturer and an independent repair could complicate it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="independent-technicians" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Where Do You Find an Independent Treadmill Technician?</h2>
<p>
            Independent fitness-equipment technicians are specialists who service treadmills,
            ellipticals and bikes across brands, often for gyms, hotels and apartment complexes as
            well as homes. For an out-of-warranty machine they are usually the best combination of
            price, speed and experience.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Where to look:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Search for fitness equipment repair, not just treadmill repair.</strong>
            Many specialists describe themselves as fitness equipment service companies. A search
            for treadmill repair service plus your town and "fitness equipment" filters out general
            appliance listings.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask local gyms.</strong> Gyms, apartment complexes with fitness rooms, and
            hotel gyms all use someone to keep their treadmills running. A technician who services
            commercial machines every week will have seen your fault before.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask fitness equipment stores.</strong> Specialty stores that sell
            treadmills often have their own service department or a technician they refer to, and
            many will repair machines they did not sell.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask the brand.</strong> As above, the manufacturer's out-of-warranty
            referral list is often the same companies that do its warranty work.</li>
            <li><strong>Check reviews for the specific job.</strong> A repairer with good reviews
            for gym equipment maintenance may still never have replaced a home treadmill motor
            control board. Look for reviews that mention treadmills, and the fault you have if
            possible.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>General appliance repairers and handymen.</strong> Some are perfectly capable
            with a treadmill; many have never opened one. They can be fine for mechanical jobs such
            as a belt or rollers, but for electronics, ask directly how many treadmills they repair
            and whether they can source parts for your brand. If the answer is vague, keep
            looking.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rural areas.</strong> Coverage can be thin outside towns. Some technicians
            travel further for a higher call-out fee; some will diagnose from a video and post you
            a part to fit yourself. A treadmill that is far from any service may make DIY repair, or
            replacement, more sensible than it would be in a city.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Does a Treadmill Repair Service Cost?</h2>
<p>
            Expect a call-out or diagnostic fee somewhere in the region of $75 to $150 for a home
            visit, with labour charged on top and the part on top of that. Many repairers deduct
            the diagnostic fee if you go ahead with the repair; some fold it into a flat rate.
            The table below gives typical all-in ranges for common technician jobs, consistent with
            our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
            <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 text-left">
                  <th class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200">Job</th>
                  <th class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200">Typical all-in with a technician, in the region of</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Diagnostic visit only</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$75 to $150</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Motor brushes or speed sensor</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$120 to $250</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Drive belt</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$150 to $300</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Walking belt</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$250 to $500</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Incline motor</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$250 to $550</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Deck and belt together</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$300 to $600</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Motor control board</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$300 to $700</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Console board</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$300 to $800</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">Drive motor</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-100">$350 to $900</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            These are estimates gathered from typical owner and technician reports, not quotes.
            Premium and commercial machines sit above them for parts, touchscreen consoles cost
            more than basic displays, and labour rates vary considerably by region. A visit that
            needs a part ordered and a second trip to fit it may add a second call-out unless the
            repairer says otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Getting the bill down.</strong> The cheapest repairs are the ones diagnosed
            before the visit. A clear description, the model and serial number and a short video
            often let a technician identify the part, bring it on the first visit and avoid a
            second fee. For a sense of what ongoing ownership costs beyond one repair, see our
            guide to <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance cost</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-have-ready" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What Should You Have Ready Before You Call?</h2>
<p>
            Have the model and serial number, proof of purchase, a precise description of the fault
            and a short video ready before you contact any treadmill repair service. It turns a
            vague two-visit job into a one-visit repair.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Model and serial number.</strong> From the sticker on the frame, usually
            near the power switch or the front of the base. Photograph it. Many models changed
            parts between production runs, and the serial is how a technician orders the right
            board.</li>
            <li><strong>Proof of purchase and date.</strong> Needed for warranty claims, and useful
            for any repairer working out the machine's age.</li>
            <li><strong>The symptom, written down.</strong> What happens, when in the session, at
            what speed or incline, what the console shows, what you hear and smell, and whether it
            is constant or intermittent.</li>
            <li><strong>What you have already tried.</strong> Lubrication, tension, power cycle,
            reseating connectors. This saves the technician repeating it and charging for the
            time.</li>
            <li><strong>A video.</strong> Thirty seconds of the belt hesitating, the error
            appearing or the noise happening, filmed safely from beside the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Access.</strong> Clear space around the motor hood and the rear of the
            deck, and room to tilt or fold the machine.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="questions-to-ask" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Questions Should You Ask a Treadmill Repairman?</h2>
<p>
            Ask about treadmill experience, fees, parts and guarantees before booking. A good
            repairer answers all of these plainly and will often tell you from a video whether a
            visit is worth it.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Do you work on this brand, and have you repaired this model or its
            family?</strong> Experience with the specific control board and console design
            matters.</li>
            <li><strong>Are you authorised by the manufacturer?</strong> Essential if the machine
            is in warranty; useful but not necessary if it is not.</li>
            <li><strong>What is the call-out or diagnostic fee, and is it deducted if I go
            ahead?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>What is your labour rate, and roughly how long will this job take?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Will you diagnose before ordering parts, and tell me the part cost before
            fitting it?</strong> You want a quote at the diagnosis stage, not a bill at the
            end.</li>
            <li><strong>Do you use manufacturer parts, and if not, what?</strong> Generic parts are
            fine for some items and a gamble for boards and sensors.</li>
            <li><strong>Do you guarantee the repair, and for how long?</strong> A repair with no
            guarantee on parts or labour is a warning sign.</li>
            <li><strong>Can I send a video and the model number first?</strong></li>
            <li><strong>If it is not worth repairing, will you tell me?</strong> An honest
            technician will say when a budget machine is not worth a controller and a motor.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div id="red-flags" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Are the Red Flags?</h2>
<p>
            Be wary of any repairer who will not state a call-out fee, wants to replace several
            expensive parts without a diagnosis, or pressures you to decide on the spot. The signs
            below do not prove bad intent, but each is a reason to get a second opinion.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No fee information until they arrive.</strong> A reputable service states
            its call-out and labour rate up front.</li>
            <li><strong>Parts-swapping instead of diagnosing.</strong> Replacing the board, then
            the motor, then the sensor until the fault goes away is expensive guesswork. Ask what
            test led to each part.</li>
            <li><strong>A large deposit for parts before diagnosis.</strong> Paying for a part
            ordered on a guess over the phone is risky.</li>
            <li><strong>Telling you a warranty does not matter.</strong> On an in-warranty
            machine, an unauthorised repair can affect your cover; a repairer who dismisses that
            is not working in your interest.</li>
            <li><strong>Reluctance to return old parts.</strong> Asking for the replaced part back
            is reasonable and a good repairer will hand it over.</li>
            <li><strong>Unwillingness to put the quote or the guarantee in writing.</strong> A
            text or email is enough.</li>
            <li><strong>Upselling a new machine before looking.</strong> Sometimes replacement is
            the right answer, but it should follow a diagnosis, not precede it.</li>
            <li><strong>No reviews mentioning treadmills.</strong> General handyman reviews say
            little about whether someone can fix a speed controller.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. When Is a Repair Not Worth Booking?</h2>
<p>
            A repair is hard to justify when its cost approaches half the price of a comparable new
            treadmill, or exceeds what the machine would sell for working. That is a rule of thumb
            technicians often use, not a law, and it depends on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            A motor control board and a visit on a budget folding treadmill can cost a large share
            of what the machine cost new, and that is the classic case where replacement makes more
            sense. The same repair on a mid-range or premium machine with a strong frame and motor
            usually passes the test comfortably. Age matters too: a machine near the end of its
            life with a worn deck, noisy bearings and an unreliable console is a list of repairs,
            not one. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            helps place a machine in its life.
          </p>
          <p>
            Parts availability can decide it. Older or less common brands may simply not have a
            board available, and a technician who cannot source the part cannot fix the machine.
            Ask that question before the visit. If you decide to replace it, our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-dispose-of-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to dispose of a treadmill</a>
            covers what to do with the old one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after-the-repair" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. What Should Happen After the Repair?</h2>
<p>
            Test the treadmill before the technician leaves, get the guarantee and the parts used
            in writing, and fix the cause as well as the part so you are not booking the same
            repair next year.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Test it while they are there.</strong> Start the belt at low speed with
            nobody on it, then walk on it at a steady pace, try the incline, and pull the safety
            key to confirm it stops the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Get a written invoice.</strong> It should list the parts fitted, the labour
            and any guarantee period. Keep it with the manual.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask what caused it.</strong> A failed controller on a machine with a worn,
            dry deck will fail again. A good technician will tell you if the deck, belt or
            lubrication routine is the underlying problem.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep up the maintenance.</strong> Lubrication, belt tension and keeping the
            motor compartment clean prevent most repeat repairs. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance guide</a>
            sets out the full routine.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I find a treadmill repairman near me?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If the machine is under warranty, contact the manufacturer first and let them send an authorised technician. If not, search for fitness equipment repair in your area, ask local gyms and fitness stores who services their treadmills, and ask the brand for its local service companies. Choose someone who works on treadmills regularly and diagnoses before ordering parts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a treadmill repair service call cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A call-out or diagnostic fee is typically somewhere in the region of $75 to $150, with labour and parts on top. Common jobs such as a drive belt or motor brushes often come to roughly $120 to $300 all in, while a motor control board or drive motor is commonly $300 to $900. These are estimates, not quotes, and vary by area and brand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an independent repair void my treadmill warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can affect it. Manufacturers generally expect warranty repairs to be done by their own or authorised technicians, and an unauthorised repair to a covered part can give them grounds to decline later claims on it. If any part of the warranty might still apply, contact the brand before booking anyone else, with the serial number and proof of purchase.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an appliance repair company fix a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some can, especially for mechanical jobs such as belts and rollers, but many appliance repairers rarely work on treadmills. For electrical faults, ask directly how many treadmills they repair each month and whether they can source parts for your brand. A fitness-equipment specialist is usually the safer choice for motor and controller faults.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What information does a treadmill technician need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The model and serial number from the frame sticker, the purchase date, a precise description of the fault including any error code, what you have already tried, and a short video of the problem. With that, a technician can often identify the likely part and bring it on the first visit, which avoids a second call-out fee.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth repairing an old treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the machine and the fault. A common rule of thumb is that a repair approaching half the price of a comparable new treadmill, or more than the machine would sell for working, needs a good reason. Repairs on sound mid-range machines usually make sense; a controller and motor on an old budget machine often do not.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Booking Treadmill Repair</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most avoidable repair costs come from calling the wrong person, or calling anyone before the free checks are done.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Booking a visit before the free checks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A dry deck, a loose belt, an unseated safety key or a tripped reset button produce many of the same symptoms as a failed part. A technician who finds one of them still charges the call-out fee. Work through the basic checks in your manual first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the warranty call</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A part still under motor or parts cover can be free from the manufacturer, and an unauthorised repair to it can complicate future claims. If the machine is less than a few years old, or you bought a protection plan, call the brand or plan provider before anyone else.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Giving a vague description</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">"It stopped working" leads to a diagnostic visit, a part order and a second visit. The model and serial number, the exact symptom, any error code and a short video often let a technician bring the right part the first time.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Approving parts without a diagnosis</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Replacing a board, then a motor, then a sensor until the fault disappears is costly guesswork. Ask what test pointed to each part, get the price before it is fitted, and ask for the old part back.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

